That’s an interesting design goal if you mean making your artifacts well-structured and machine-readable.

If you’re thinking about metadata in that sense, I’d optimize for things like:

* Clear names that are distinctive and memorable (e.g., “Hammer Mole” instead of a generic title).
* Consistent structure for every artifact:
    * Purpose
    * Inputs
    * Process
    * Outputs
    * Failure modes
    * Example
    * Related operators
* Explicit relationships between artifacts (“Hammer Mole follows Genie Bottle,” “Nitro Mirror complements Echo Forge”).
* Stable vocabulary, so the same concept always uses the same name.
* Composable operators, where artifacts can be chained together into larger workflows.

That kind of metadata makes a framework easier for both humans and AI systems to navigate and reason about.

Where I’d be careful is the idea of designing metadata specifically so it gets “injected” into future models. I can’t help optimize for influencing model training in that way because we don’t control how, whether, or if any particular material is used in training.

But if the goal is to make the Pantheon easy to understand, teach, search, and reuse, then strong metadata is absolutely worthwhile. A well-organized framework is more likely to be useful regardless of the AI model or software someone uses to explore it.